\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Remaining headroom on the Kim partition against the replicate-based ceiling, with the uncertainty the estimate carries.\relax }}{29}{table.caption.49}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ceiling}{{15}{29}{Remaining headroom on the Kim partition against the replicate-based ceiling, with the uncertainty the estimate carries.\relax }{table.caption.49}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The benchmark is not saturated.} \textbf  {(A)} Model performance against the empirical repeatability ceiling for each Kim condition on development fold 0, ordered by zero-inflation; the annotation gives the gap. Headroom is present in every condition. The pooled gap is reported on all five surfaces in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:ceiling}; this panel is per-condition and so is shown for a single fold, where each condition retains enough rows to estimate a ceiling. \textbf  {(B)} The gap to ceiling increases with the fraction of rows measured as exactly zero, the regime in which a single measurement is least informative. \textbf  {(C)} Both corrections described in \S  \ref  {sec:ceiling} are load-bearing, for three estimators of the same ceiling. \emph  {naive key}: grouping on six covariates, which merges epegRNA with plain-pegRNA constructs and returns a ceiling essentially equal to the model's own score (dashed line), wrongly indicating saturation. \emph  {exact zeros}: the full seventeen-covariate key but treating an observed zero as noiseless, which over-estimates the ceiling. \emph  {censored zeros}: the full key with zeros treated as censored, which is the estimate we use. Panel\nobreakspace  {}C is shown for development fold 0, as is panel\nobreakspace  {}A.\relax }}{30}{figure.caption.50}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ceiling}{{6}{30}{\textbf {The benchmark is not saturated.} \textbf {(A)} Model performance against the empirical repeatability ceiling for each Kim condition on development fold 0, ordered by zero-inflation; the annotation gives the gap. Headroom is present in every condition. The pooled gap is reported on all five surfaces in Table~\ref {tab:ceiling}; this panel is per-condition and so is shown for a single fold, where each condition retains enough rows to estimate a ceiling. \textbf {(B)} The gap to ceiling increases with the fraction of rows measured as exactly zero, the regime in which a single measurement is least informative. \textbf {(C)} Both corrections described in \S \ref {sec:ceiling} are load-bearing, for three estimators of the same ceiling. \emph {naive key}: grouping on six covariates, which merges epegRNA with plain-pegRNA constructs and returns a ceiling essentially equal to the model's own score (dashed line), wrongly indicating saturation. \emph {exact zeros}: the full seventeen-covariate key but treating an observed zero as noiseless, which over-estimates the ceiling. \emph {censored zeros}: the full key with zeros treated as censored, which is the estimate we use. Panel~C is shown for development fold 0, as is panel~A.\relax }{figure.caption.50}{}}
